/* The settings overlay (gear + panel) is styled entirely in settings.uss. */
@import url("/Assets/UI/Common/settings.uss");

/* ================================================================================================
 * WHAT ONE AUTHORED PIXEL IS. Read this before trusting any number in any sheet in this project.
 *
 * THE PANEL IS 1080 AUTHORED px TALL ON EVERY DEVICE, AND ALWAYS EXACTLY 1080.
 * All three PanelSettings run ScaleWithScreenSize against a 1920x1080 reference matched on HEIGHT
 * (m_ScaleMode: 2, m_ScreenMatchMode: 0, m_Match: 1), so `scale = screenHeight / 1080` and the
 * panel's own height is that constant. Its WIDTH is 1080 * aspect — 2400 on a 20:9 phone, ~1440 on
 * a 4:3 tablet — and is the only dimension with any variation left in it.
 *
 * SO AN AUTHORED px IS A FRACTION OF THE SCREEN, NOT A LENGTH. It has no physical size until you
 * name a device. On a Pixel 5a (1080 px across 2.60" of glass) it is 0.148 Android dp, so:
 *
 *     an authored 56  =  21dp of finger        Android's 48dp touch floor  =  125 authored px
 *
 * `referenceDpi: 160` is still in the assets and is INERT — ScaleWithScreenSize never reads it.
 *
 * IT WAS NOT ALWAYS THIS. Between 99b179b1 and 02048077 (2026-09-01) the panels ran
 * ConstantPhysicalSize at referenceDpi 160, where one authored px WAS one Android dp and a
 * landscape phone panel was 914 x 411. EVERY NUMBER AND EVERY COMMENT IN THIS PROJECT AUTHORED
 * BEFORE 2026-09-01 IS IN THOSE UNITS and reads about 2.6x too small. A comment that says "411dp"
 * or "one authored px is one Android dp" is describing a panel that no longer exists; where one
 * has been left standing deliberately it says so, because the DECISION it records is usually still
 * sound even though the arithmetic under it moved.
 *
 * HOW TO CHECK RATHER THAN ASSUME: `Kingdom/Dev/Game View → Pixel 5a` sets the Game View to
 * 2400x1080, which now reproduces the device exactly (same resolution, same panel — there is no
 * dpi left to correct for), then `DeviceLayoutRig.Measure(...)` prints worldBound off the live
 * tree. Issue #349 is the full account.
 * ============================================================================================= */
.emb-root {
    --space-1: 4px;
    --space-2: 8px;
    --space-3: 12px;
    --space-4: 16px;
    --space-6: 24px;
    --space-8: 32px;
    --space-12: 48px;
    --space-16: 64px;

    /* How far anything anchored to a screen SIDE must sit from it. HORIZONTAL ONLY --
       see the axis table below, which is the whole point of the number.
       Not part of the 4px spacing run above -- it is not a design rhythm, it is a
       hardware clearance, and it is deliberately blunt.

       52 IS WRONG AS OF 2026-09-01 AND ISSUE #350 OWNS THE FIX. Everything below is
       correct arithmetic in the OLD units, where an authored px was an Android dp. It is
       not any more (see the block at the top of this file), so the cutout's 136 DEVICE px
       needs ~136 AUTHORED px on a 1080p phone and this token supplies 52. Content anchored
       to a side is being clipped by real hardware. Left at 52 deliberately: moving it moves
       eight screens' horizontal insets at once and that belongs in one change with eyes on
       a device, not as a side effect of #349's vocabulary sweep.

       THE UNIT CHANGED UNDER THIS NUMBER TWICE, and that is the whole story of its history.
       Authored as 150 when the panels scaled by pixel count, so 150 authored px was about
       150 DEVICE px and cleared the cutout. At referenceDpi 160 one authored px is one dp,
       so 150 became 150dp = 394 device px -- 73% of a 1080-tall landscape screen (#243).

       52 is the measured value, not an estimate. Read off a Pixel 5a over USB, 2026-08-23:

           ROTATION_90 : nonDecorInsets=[136,0][0,63]   <- 136 device px on the LEFT
           ROTATION_270: nonDecorInsets=[0,0][136,63]   <- 136 device px on the RIGHT

       136 device px / 2.625 px per dp = 51.8dp, so 52 clears it and 50 does not (50dp is
       131 device px, five short). Note the side FLIPS with rotation direction, which is why
       this is spent on BOTH sides rather than the one the API happens to name: Unity's
       Screen.safeArea reported the cutout on the LEFT while Android's mAppBounds and Sam's
       own eyes put it on the RIGHT (2026-08-21). Sit far enough in that the side stops
       mattering. A static margin right on every device beats a computed inset right on some.

       IT IS SPENT ON THE HORIZONTAL AXIS ONLY, and that is measured, not tidied. Read off
       the same Pixel 5a on 2026-08-30, in landscape (ROTATION_270), with the system bars
       hidden as they now are:

           mDisplayCutout insets = Rect(0, 0 - 136, 0)      left 0  top 0  RIGHT 136  bottom 0
           boundingRect          = Rect(2264, 0 - 2400, 145)

       So the top and bottom clearance requirement is ZERO -- there is no cutout on either,
       and the 63 device px the display frame reserves at the bottom is the GESTURE BAR,
       which 080966bc hides. Spending 52 there cost 104dp of a 411dp landscape panel, a
       QUARTER OF THE SCREEN, for a hazard that does not exist on that axis. It is why the
       main menu's build stamp floated three quarters of the way up the screen and why the
       leaderboard needed its own modifier to reach full height.

       A screen that wants a vertical gutter authors one; the classes below no longer
       impose it. That is the same relation the horizontal axis already had -- margin
       COMPOSES with the offset the screen authored, so `.mm-footer { bottom: 24 }` keeps
       meaning 24 and this adds nothing to it any more.

       RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if the game ever ships with the system bars VISIBLE
       (PlayerSettings.Android.requestedVisibleInsets != 0, see 080966bc), the bottom
       gesture bar is back and .emb-edge/.emb-bleed need their bottom clearance back at
       24dp (63 device px). Nothing else re-derives this.

       These two classes were commented out on effect-model-merge and restored 2026-08-23.
       While they were dead, `character-create` -- which carries .emb-bleed -- ran edge to
       edge, putting "Choose your hero" under the status bar and Cancel under the gesture
       bar. The classes are not decoration; they are the only thing insetting that modal.

       RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if a device with a cutout deeper than 136 device px is supported,
       or referenceDpi moves off 160, recompute this. It is the only place it is written
       down, and tooling/scripts/audit_edge_safety.py fails if either class loses its body. */
    --edge-safe: 52px;

    /* THE SMALLEST A CONTROL A FINGER HAS TO HIT MAY BE.

       ONE AUTHORED px IS ONE PANEL px, AND THE PANEL IS ALWAYS 1080 TALL. The panel runs
       ScaleWithScreenSize against 1920x1080 matched on height, so `scale = screenHeight /
       1080` and an authored px is a constant fraction of the screen's height on every
       device. That is what makes a single number defensible: 56 is 5.2% of the short axis
       whether the screen is 1080p or 1440p, so there is nothing left for it to step with.

       IT IS NO LONGER 48 = ANDROID's 48dp. Under ConstantPhysicalSize (retired #349) an
       authored px WAS one Android dp and 48 was literally Google's guidance. It is not any
       more, and the comparison is gone rather than adjusted: 56 is what actually rendered
       for every device once `.emb-sw-expanded` became the only reachable screen class, and
       it is ~21 physical dp on a Pixel 5a's 411dp of height — comfortably above the floor.

       A token rather than 56 written thirteen times, because thirteen copies is how the
       old ones drifted: the sweep for #244 found .ms-gear at 44, .db-chip at 32,
       .hero-tile-badge at 34 and .new-character-button at 40, every one of them authored
       under a scale where the number meant nothing checkable.

       USE THIS, NOT A BARE PIXEL, on anything pressed. tooling/scripts/audit_touch_targets.py
       reads this token's own value and flags a bare px below it on any selector it can prove
       is interactive.

       UI Toolkit sizes BORDER-BOX, so this is the touch target with no adjustment for the
       rule's own border-width. */
    --touch-min: 56px;

    /* THE TYPE SCALE — ONE STEP, NOT A RUN (#349, #247 closed with it).

       These were the `.emb-sw-expanded` values until 2026-09-03. There were three steps
       keyed by screen class, and after the panel moved to ScaleWithScreenSize the panel's
       short axis became a constant 1080 on every device, so `expanded` was the only branch
       that could ever fire and the other two were dead. Folding the live branch into the
       base leaves rendering identical and takes the dead vocabulary with it.

       DO NOT REINTRODUCE A PER-DEVICE STEP HERE without re-reading #349. The panel already
       scales uniformly with screen height; a token that ALSO steps would be scaling twice.
       If a future breakpoint is wanted it has to key on the panel's WIDTH, which is the only
       axis that still varies (1440 authored px on a 4:3 tablet, 2400 on a 20:9 phone). */
    --type-display: 44px;
    --type-h1: 36px;
    --type-h2: 28px;
    --type-body: 24px;
    --type-label: 22px;
    --type-caption: 18px;
    --surface-0: rgb(11, 14, 19);
    --surface-1: rgb(20, 25, 34);
    --surface-2: rgb(30, 37, 48);
    --surface-3: rgb(42, 51, 64);
    --ink-1: rgb(237, 241, 246);
    --ink-2: rgb(154, 167, 182);
    --accent: rgb(240, 166, 60);
    --positive: rgb(75, 208, 123);
    --negative: rgb(229, 72, 77);
    --warning: rgb(245, 166, 35);
    --info: rgb(76, 154, 255);
    --mana: rgb(61, 165, 245);
    --rarity-common: rgb(154, 167, 182);
    --rarity-rare: rgb(61, 123, 245);
    --rarity-epic: rgb(162, 75, 229);
    --rarity-legendary: rgb(240, 166, 60);
    --class-neutral: rgb(138, 143, 152);
    --class-emberward: rgb(229, 96, 42);
    --class-tidecaller: rgb(31, 138, 143);
    --class-gravekeep: rgb(126, 107, 158);
    --class-verdant: rgb(75, 154, 84);
    --class-tempest: rgb(16, 14, 150);
    --class-clockwright: rgb(114, 101, 61);
    --class-stonewarden: rgb(107, 129, 141);
    --class-nightveil: rgb(35, 5, 78);
}

/* ── Hardware edge clearance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   --edge-safe above is the distance anything anchored to a screen SIDE keeps
   from it. It reaches a screen through these two classes and nowhere else — no
   screen restates the value, so retuning it is one line in one file.

   BOTH SPEND IT AS `0 var(--edge-safe)` — LEFT AND RIGHT ONLY. The cutout is on
   a side and the top and bottom measure zero; the derivation and the retirement
   trigger are on the token above. The zero is written into the shorthand rather
   than left to a longhand so the one property still owns all four sides, which
   is what PASS 6 of audit_edge_safety.py reasons about.

   MARGIN, not padding, for chrome. A parent's padding does not reach a
   position:absolute child, and every piece of screen chrome is absolutely
   positioned. Measured on a Pixel 5a, 2026-08-21: a parent padding of 136 moved
   the identity plate, the footer and the shard counter by exactly 0; the same
   136 as margin moved all three by 136. Margin also COMPOSES with the offset the
   screen authored, so `left: var(--space-6)` keeps meaning 24 and this adds to
   it — the design gutter sits inside the hardware clearance rather than being
   replaced by it.

   Applied per element rather than to every child of .emb-root, because a flow
   child in a column would take the clearance on its TOP and BOTTOM too, and
   three of those on a 1080-unit panel eats the screen. */
.emb-edge {
    margin: 0 var(--edge-safe);
}

/* A layer that must PAINT to the panel edge — a scrim, a modal backdrop — spans
   the panel and insets its own CONTENT instead. This is why one class serves both
   the bare scrims and the modal containers: paint to the edge, keep content in. */
.emb-bleed {
    padding: 0 var(--edge-safe);
}

/* THE "THERE IS MORE" SIGNAL FOR A LIST WITH NO SCROLLBAR.
   Scrollbars are hidden game-wide by ruling, which leaves a half-visible last row as the only
   thing telling a player the list continues — and a row cut through its own TEXT reads as
   breakage, not as an affordance (Sam, visual audit #7: "veward" cut mid-word in the deck
   chooser). A fade over the last band turns the same clip into a deliberate edge.

   USS HAS NO GRADIENTS. Probed against the shipping module on 2026-08-30 —
   `linear-gradient` and `radial-gradient` appear ZERO times in
   6000.5.1f1/Editor/Data/Managed/UnityEngine/UnityEngine.UIElementsModule.dll (the same probe
   returns 0 for `box-sizing`, which is independently known to be absent). So the ramp is a
   64x64 texture, white with a smoothstep alpha, and each USE TINTS IT to whatever surface sits
   behind that list — the tint multiplies, so white is the only authoring that works for all of
   them. A use with no tint fades to white and is obviously wrong on sight.

   ITS .meta's PLATFORM OVERRIDES ARE UNITY'S, NOT OURS, and trying to own them is wasted work.
   The file was authored RGBA32/uncompressed on Standalone, Android and iOS to keep block
   compression off a vertical alpha ramp — and Unity's first import silently rewrote all three
   back to its own defaults (2048 / DXT5 / ASTC, compression on). A hand-edited platform override
   block in a .meta does not survive the importer; only the Inspector or an AssetPostprocessor
   makes one stick. So the shipped texture IS compressed. Watch this fade for banding on a device
   before assuming otherwise, and if it bands, fix it through the importer rather than the YAML.

   IT IS NOT SELF-POSITIONING. `position: absolute` inside a ScrollView scrolls WITH the content,
   so this element is a sibling of the ScrollView inside a `position: relative` frame, never a
   child of it. `.deck-chooser-frame` in collection.uss is the worked example.

   RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if the no-visible-scrollbars ruling is ever lifted, this class and
   ui_scroll_fade.png go with it — the fade exists only because the scrollbar cannot. */
.emb-scroll-fade {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    height: 28px;
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/Chrome/ui_scroll_fade.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: stretch-to-fill;
}

/* ---- Class -> hero-power art -------------------------------------------------------------------
 * One image per class, and THE FILE NAMES DO NOT MATCH THE ENUM. `tempest` is `storm_hp`,
 * `clockwright` is `clock_hp`, `nightveil` is `night_hp`, `gravekeep` is `grave_hp`,
 * `tidecaller` is `tide_hp`, `stonewarden` is `stone_hp`, `emberward` is `ember_hp`. Nothing
 * derives one from the other and nothing can -- `storm` is not a prefix of `tempest` -- so this
 * block IS the map, and it lives here rather than in a screen's sheet because two screens need it:
 * the collection's class rail and the character-create picker's plate.
 *
 * Keyed on the ENUM MEMBER LOWERCASED, like every other per-class token run in this file. A class
 * added to the enum needs a row here or its glyph is simply absent -- there is no fallback, by
 * design, because a wrong hero power drawn confidently is worse than a missing one.
 *
 * The selector name is the collection's, kept deliberately: moving the rules was meant to remove a
 * copy, not to force an edit to CollectionScreen.uxml and CollectionController on the way past.
 * RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if the collection's class rail is ever removed, rename these to something
 * that does not say "tab" -- the character-create picker will be the only caller left. */
.class-tab_art-emberward {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/ember_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-tempest {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/storm_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-stonewarden {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/stone_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-nightveil {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/night_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-verdant {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/verdant_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-gravekeep {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/grave_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-clockwright {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/clock_hp.png");
}
.class-tab_art-tidecaller {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/hptabs/tide_hp.png");
}

/* ── SCREEN CLASSES ARE GONE (#247, retired by #349 on 2026-09-03) ─────────────────────────
   `.emb-sw-compact` / `-medium` / `-expanded` and the ScreenClass.cs that applied them were
   deleted here. Their values now live in the base `.emb-root` block at the top of this file.

   WHY, in one line: the panel stopped being sized in physical units, so the axis they keyed
   on stopped varying. `KingdomPanelSettings` runs ScaleWithScreenSize against 1920x1080
   matched on HEIGHT, which makes the panel's authored height exactly 1080 on every device
   and in every Game View. The classes keyed on the SHORT axis with Material 3's thresholds
   (600 / 840), so 1080 landed in `expanded` always and unconditionally — one branch of three
   was reachable and the other two were dead code that read as live design.

   IF YOU WANT BREAKPOINTS BACK, key them on the panel's WIDTH. That is the only axis with
   any variation left: 1080 * aspect, so ~1440 authored px on a 4:3 tablet against 2400 on a
   20:9 phone. Height cannot tell you anything — it is a constant.

   TWO THINGS THAT SURVIVED THE DELETION, because they are about USS and not about dp:

   1. USS SILENTLY DROPS calc(). Measured in a live panel 2026-08-25:
          calc(var(--probe-base) * 2)   resolved width = 4000.00   <- declaration DROPPED
          var(--probe-base)             resolved width = 10.00
      4000 was the panel width, i.e. the element fell back to `auto`. So a token run can
      never be `calc(var(--type-body) * var(--ui-scale))`; every step has to be written out.

   2. --space-* IS NOT A TYPE TOKEN and never stepped with them. Several layout solves are
      written against it — LayoutHeroGrid's HeroGutter and HeroChrome are literal copies of
      --space-2 and its border — so it moves only with its own evidence. */

/* ================================================================================================
 * NO VISIBLE SCROLLBARS, ANYWHERE (Sam, 2026-08-01 ruling — [[no-visible-scrollbars-ruling]]).
 * Wheel, touch-drag and keyboard scrolling all still work; this hides only the track+thumb
 * chrome, which reads as generic UI Toolkit default rather than as this game's visual language.
 *
 * ONE RULE REPLACED SIX (#161, 2026-08-25). The same block had been written out per ScrollView in
 * character-roster.uss (x2), collection.uss, mainmenu.uss and shop.uss — and FIVE OF THE SIX WERE
 * INCOMPLETE. Only #roster-scroll listed all five classes; .shop-scroll listed one. A horizontal
 * scroller was visible in the collection, the shop and the deck-image strip, because each copy
 * hid whichever scroller its author happened to have on screen that day.
 *
 * THE CLASS IS DOUBLED ON PURPOSE. `.unity-scroller.unity-scroller` matches exactly what
 * `.unity-scroller` matches, but scores (0,2,0) instead of (0,1,0), so it beats the default
 * runtime theme without needing an ancestor. An ancestor-scoped form (`.emb-root ...`) would miss
 * ConnectionFailedScreen.uxml, whose root is .mm-modal and which is built into the panel at
 * runtime rather than under a screen root.
 *
 * THIS RULE IS A BACKSTOP, NOT THE MECHANISM — 2026-08-30. It only bites where content never
 * overflows. `ScrollView` writes its scroller's `display` as an INLINE style driven by
 * `scrollerVisibility` (default `Auto`), and inline beats every USS selector: the doubling below,
 * an id selector, anything — USS has no `!important`. So the moment a surface actually overflows,
 * the scroller comes back and this rule is powerless. Consolidating six copies into one (#161) was
 * right; it did not make any of them work.
 *
 * THE MECHANISM IS THE UXML ATTRIBUTE: `vertical-scroller-visibility="Hidden"` /
 * `horizontal-scroller-visibility="Hidden"` on the `<ui:ScrollView>`, which sets what the internal
 * logic reads. Every new ScrollView gets both from the start. A `ListView` does not expose the
 * property — set it on its inner ScrollView in C#, as CollectionController does.
 *
 * NEVER read this rule's presence as evidence a surface is covered. That read is what let the bug
 * survive two fixes; the shop shipped a visible scroller on a build four days after #161 landed.
 *
 * RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if the no-visible-scrollbars ruling is ever relaxed for one surface, this
 * rule is the thing that has to gain an exception — do NOT re-add a per-id copy, which is how it
 * drifted into six divergent versions the first time.
 * ================================================================================================ */
.unity-scroller.unity-scroller,
.unity-scroller--vertical.unity-scroller--vertical,
.unity-scroller--horizontal.unity-scroller--horizontal,
.unity-scroll-view__vertical-scroller.unity-scroll-view__vertical-scroller,
.unity-scroll-view__horizontal-scroller.unity-scroll-view__horizontal-scroller {
    display: none;
}

/* ================================================================================================
 * EVERY BUTTON IS CHROME (Sam, 2026-09-30). Slate by default, ember with `.emb-primary`.
 *
 * ONE-CLASS SPECIFICITY ON PURPOSE. This sheet is linked FIRST on every screen, so a screen's own
 * one-class rule that sets `background-image` (a gear, a tab strip, a plate) still wins the tie and
 * keeps its art. A button that must NOT be chrome says `background-image: none` in its own rule.
 *
 * A SCREEN RULE THAT SETS `background-color` ON A CHROME BUTTON BRINGS THE SQUARE CORNERS BACK.
 * The fill paints under the sprite's transparent corners. Tint the sprite instead
 * (`-unity-background-image-tint-color`), as `.roster-button--danger` does.
 *
 * THE CORNER FIX is the state block below `.unity-button:disabled`: it clears the fill on every
 * hover/press/focus state directly, so nothing paints under the sprite's clear corners.
 * ================================================================================================ */
.unity-button {
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/Chrome/ui_button_secondary_slate.png");
    -unity-slice-left: 64;
    -unity-slice-right: 64;
    -unity-slice-top: 36;
    -unity-slice-bottom: 36;
    -unity-slice-scale: 0.5px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgb(232, 236, 242);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    transition-property: -unity-background-image-tint-color, opacity, translate;
    transition-duration: 0.1s;
    transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}
.unity-button:hover {
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
}
.unity-button:active {
    translate: 0 1px;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgb(205, 211, 220);
}
.unity-button:disabled {
    opacity: 0.42;
}

/* THE GREY SQUARE (Sam, 2026-09-30, read off the UI Debugger): a hover/press/focus fill paints under
 * the chrome sprite's clear corners. Cleared on every state, at `.unity-button:hover:enabled`'s
 * specificity. A plate that WANTS a fill on a button pairs its own rule with `.unity-button`
 * (`.hero-choice.unity-button:hover`) and loads after this sheet. */
.unity-button:hover,
.unity-button:hover:enabled,
.unity-button:active,
.unity-button:active:enabled,
.unity-button:hover:active,
.unity-button:hover:active:enabled,
.unity-button:focus,
.unity-button:focus:enabled,
.unity-button:disabled {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
}

/* After `.unity-button`: same specificity, so order decides. */
.emb-primary {
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/Chrome/ui_button_primary_ember.png");
    -unity-slice-top: 34;
    -unity-slice-bottom: 34;
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgb(255, 236, 205);
    color: rgb(250, 230, 193);
}
.emb-primary:active {
    -unity-background-image-tint-color: rgb(230, 206, 170);
}

/* THE CANONICAL BACK BUTTON (Sam, 2026-09-27). One visual definition for every screen's own
 * corner Back control, replacing four independent copies that had drifted: Collection/Shop's
 * `col-button`+`chrome-button` combo (which only rendered correctly on Collection -- Shop never
 * loaded `collection.uss`, so its `col-button`/`col-button--ghost` classes matched nothing and
 * Unity's default button background showed through the chrome sprite's transparent corners),
 * the roster's `#back-button` ID-selector escape hatch fighting `.roster-button`'s own sizing,
 * and the pack vault's flat-color `.vault-back` with no chrome at all.
 *
 * Apply THIS CLASS ALONE alongside the functional `.emb-back` marker (BackNavigation.cs). It
 * carries its own size and position and takes its chrome from `.unity-button` above, so it never
 * needs a screen-local override.
 *
 * height 68 matches `.ms-gear` (settings.uss), the opposite bottom corner, so the two read
 * as one row. That is 12 over `--touch-min`'s floor, matching this session's "make corner nav
 * bigger" pass (back, Collection's Decks button, the settings gear) -- Filters was left alone.
 *
 * TEXT IS PLAIN "Back", NO ARROW GLYPH. U+2190 rendered as a double dash on the roster screen
 * (font-coverage gamble, same one `.col-filter-ribbon__chevron`'s comment in collection.uss
 * warns off for the same reason) -- since the glyph is a bet against the SAME shared font
 * everywhere, skip it everywhere rather than per screen.
 *
 * Mirrors `.ms-gear`'s bottom-right anchor (settings.uss) on the opposite side, same
 * var(--space-6) offset both axes, `margin: 0 var(--edge-safe)` composing with it the same way
 * `.emb-edge` does (see the comment above it) for the hardware cutout. */
.emb-back-button {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: var(--space-6);
    left: var(--space-6);
    margin: 0 var(--edge-safe);
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 68px;
    height: 68px;
    padding: 0 var(--space-6);
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}
/* CLEAR CORNERS in every state (Sam, 2026-09-29). Paired with `.unity-button` on purpose: it
 * outranks any screen's own button rules. The button rules above should make this redundant; it
 * stays until that is seen on a device. */
.emb-back-button.unity-button,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:hover,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:hover:enabled,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:active,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:active:enabled,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:focus,
.emb-back-button.unity-button:disabled {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-radius: 0;
}

/* Menu buttons: Modern Antiqua (Sam, 2026-09-30). Every <ui:Button> carries .unity-button, so
   this reaches all of them on every screen that loads this sheet. Doubled for specificity, the same
   trick as the scroller rules above. Bold on every button, including the ones whose own class does
   not ask for it: Modern Antiqua ships one weight, so this is UI Toolkit's synthetic bold, and Sam
   chose it over the regular face on sight (2026-09-30).
   The asset is built by Kingdom/Regenerate/Fonts/Menu Button Asset. */
.unity-button.unity-button {
    -unity-font-definition: url("/Assets/Art/Fonts/ModernAntiqua-Regular UITK.asset");
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}
